## Key statistics and market data

Market size: The U.S. grocery sector is valued at $912.4 billion, according to IBISWorld.

Investment activity: Deals for grocery-anchored centers totaled almost $11 billion in 2025, up 42% from 2024 (per JLL's Grocery Tracker 2026).

Tracked centers: JLL Income Property Trust's STARS database tracks 40,000 grocery-anchored retail centers nationwide.

## Company-specific growth and formats

Trader Joe's: Achieved 3% year-over-year growth for the period ending Feb. 28, outperforming the entire grocery sector by 6 percentage points. Consumer Edge noted "double-digit year-over-year growth among Gen Z shoppers."

Target: Its food-and-beverage segment generated over $24 billion in net sales during fiscal year 2025 (ended Feb. 1). The company is repositioning around differentiation and specialty items rather than competing as a traditional grocer.

Walmart: Remodeled more than 650 standard stores and Neighborhood Markets in 2026; allocated over $5.5 billion for approximately 675 store remodels in fiscal year 2026 (ended Jan. 31); operates more than 5,200 U.S. stores; planning about 20 new store openings in 2026 and early 2027.

Costco: Operates nearly 640 U.S. stores; expects 28 net new openings in fiscal year 2026 (starting Sept. 1); targets 30-plus new stores annually going forward.

Aldi: Operates more than 2,400 U.S. stores (of nearly 7,600 globally). Testing a modular store format in Aventura, Florida (starting September 2025, continuing through 2026). Opening 150 stores nationally in 2026 for its 50th U.S. anniversary.

Gelson's: Operates 27 traditional grocery stores in Southern California. Opened its second ReCharge by Gelson's micromarket (5,400 sq. ft.) in Costa Mesa, paired with EV charging. First location in Santa Ana measured 3,000 sq. ft.

Regency Centers: Developing a 71,400-square-foot Whole Foods-anchored center in Redlands, California featuring a 36,000-sq.-ft. grocery store plus four retail buildings accommodating up to 18 additional stores and a drive-thru pad site.

## Recent investment transactions

Brixton Capital: Acquired Las Palmas Village (Vons-anchored, Las Vegas) for $29.4 million (106,838 sq. ft., 95% leased); acquired Quad at Whittier (Vallarta Supermarkets-anchored, Los Angeles) for $100 million (314,593 sq. ft., 95.6% leased).

Medipower: Purchased a portfolio of seven grocery-anchored retail centers (558,000 sq. ft.) for $115 million.

Nuveen: Paid $298 million for a seven-property retail portfolio (956,865 sq. ft.), with six anchored by groceries.

Blackstone Real Estate: Acquired three largely grocery-anchored portfolios comprising 130 properties totaling $6.7 billion in 2025 and 2026.

## Market trends and consumer behavior

Traditional grocer performance: Publix and Safeway "are seeing pullbacks from every income group, with the sharpest declines among lower-income shoppers" (Consumer Edge).

Discount grocery: Growth has plateaued since mid-2025 in the Aldi and Food 4 Less sector.

Restaurant competition: Per Tillster's 2026 Phygital Index Report (surveying 2,144 diners): 29% visit fast-food chains less often; 37% visit fast-casual chains less often; 36% visit grocery stores more often; 33% visit convenience stores more frequently.

## Expert commentary

Naveen Jaggi (JLL): "The days when any grocery store could anchor a center equally well are fading." Specialty grocers and value-oriented formats are reshaping the landscape.

Michael Gunther (Consumer Edge): Shoppers gravitate toward retailers offering "a clear reason to be loyal, whether that's unbeatable value at a hard discounter or a curated private-label experience."

Cara Sylvester (Target CMO): Target aims to build "a truly distinctive grocery destination" targeting families seeking "fun, trend-forward options."
